---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { Header } from '../components/Header';
import { LightField } from '../components/LightField';
import { Diagram } from '../components/diagram/Diagram';
import { siteFlow } from '../components/diagram/siteFlow';
import { pipeline } from '../components/diagram/pipeline';
import { requestPath } from '../components/diagram/requestPath';
import { iacSplit } from '../components/diagram/iacSplit';
import { awsAccount } from '../components/diagram/awsAccount';
import { awsOrg } from '../components/diagram/awsOrg';
import { cloudflareZone } from '../components/diagram/cloudflareZone';
import { repoUrl, services, setupCheckedOn } from '../data/howItWorks';
import { site } from '../data/site';

// CloudFront serves /how-it-works by rewriting it to /how-it-works/index.html, so every link and asset is
// root-absolute or a #fragment. The claims below were checked against the live setup on setupCheckedOn.
const FLOW_MIN_SCALE = 0.58;
const DIAGRAM_MIN_SCALE = 0.6;

const toc = [
  { href: '#pipeline', label: 'From an edit to production' },
  { href: '#serving', label: 'Serving a request' },
  { href: '#iac', label: 'Infrastructure as code' },
  { href: '#aws', label: 'The AWS accounts' },
  { href: '#cloudflare', label: 'The Cloudflare account' },
  { href: '#services', label: 'Every service' },
];
---

<Base
  title={`How this site works — ${site.name}`}
  description="How sebastiansuarez.dev is built, shipped and served: Astro, GitHub Actions, OpenTofu, AWS and Cloudflare, and how the cloud accounts behind it are secured."
>
  <a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
  <div id="top" class="page">
    <LightField variant="top" />
    <Header base="/" />
    <main id="main" tabindex="-1">
      <section class="hiw-intro wrap" aria-labelledby="hiw-title">
        <div>
          <p class="hiw-kicker">Under the hood</p>
          <h1 id="hiw-title" class="enter">How this site works</h1>
          <p class="hiw-lede enter d1">
            A static site on AWS behind Cloudflare, shipped by GitHub Actions and described in OpenTofu. Here is every
            service, from a local edit to your screen, and how the AWS and Cloudflare accounts behind it are secured.
          </p>
          <p class="hiw-asof enter d1">
            Checked against the live setup on <time datetime={setupCheckedOn.iso}>{setupCheckedOn.label}</time>. The
            code is public on <a href={repoUrl}>GitHub</a>.
          </p>
          <nav class="enter d2" aria-label="On this page">
            <ul class="hiw-toc">
              {
                toc.map((item) => (
                  <li>
                    <a class="plate" href={item.href}>
                      {item.label}
                    </a>
                  </li>
                ))
              }
            </ul>
          </nav>
        </div>
        <figure class="glass-panel hiw-figure enter d3">
          <Diagram data={siteFlow} minScale={FLOW_MIN_SCALE} />
          <figcaption>From a merged pull request to your browser.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="pipeline" class="hiw-section wrap" aria-labelledby="pipeline-title">
        <div class="hiw-panel glass-panel">
          <div class="hiw-head">
            <h2 id="pipeline-title">From an edit to production</h2>
            <p>Nothing reaches the site without a pull request, two passing checks and a merge.</p>
          </div>
          <figure class="hiw-figure-inline">
            <Diagram data={pipeline} minScale={DIAGRAM_MIN_SCALE} />
            <figcaption>Two required checks gate every merge; what the merge changed decides what runs next.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <ul class="hiw-points">
            <li>
              <strong>Local.</strong> Astro builds plain HTML and CSS. React components render at build time, so no
              framework ships to your browser. Tailwind CSS 4 styles the pages, and <code>astro check</code> catches type
              errors.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Pull request.</strong> <code>main</code> only takes pull requests: no direct pushes, no force pushes,
              no deletion and no bypass. Build runs a clean install and the production build. Terraform plan runs on every
              pull request; when the infrastructure changed, it formats and validates all three stacks, plans the IAM and
              site stacks with a read-only role and posts the plans to the job summary.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Forks and bots.</strong> Pull requests from forks or Dependabot get no cloud credentials, so they can
              bump dependencies but can't change the infrastructure.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Merge.</strong> A merge that changes the site runs the deploy job in the <code>production</code>
              environment. It builds the site, assumes the deploy role with GitHub's OIDC token, syncs the files to S3 and
              invalidates CloudFront.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Caching.</strong> Hashed assets are cached for a year. Browsers revalidate HTML on every visit, and
              each deploy invalidates CloudFront, so a change is live as soon as the invalidation finishes.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Infrastructure.</strong> A merge that changes <code>infra/</code> runs two apply jobs: the IAM stack
              in the <code>infra-iam</code> environment, then the site stack in the <code>infra</code> environment. Both
              apply the tip of <code>main</code>, one run at a time, so re-running an old job can't roll anything back.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Upkeep.</strong> Dependabot opens grouped weekly updates, and push protection blocks pushes that
              contain known kinds of secrets.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="serving" class="hiw-section wrap" aria-labelledby="serving-title">
        <div class="hiw-panel glass-panel">
          <div class="hiw-head">
            <h2 id="serving-title">Serving a request</h2>
            <p>Three hops, each with one job: Cloudflare in front, CloudFront as the CDN, S3 as a private origin.</p>
          </div>
          <figure class="hiw-figure-inline">
            <Diagram data={requestPath} minScale={DIAGRAM_MIN_SCALE} />
            <figcaption>What each hop does with your request.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <ul class="hiw-points">
            <li>
              <strong>Cloudflare</strong> serves the domain's DNS, signed with DNSSEC. The apex and www are proxied, so you
              meet Cloudflare's edge: TLS 1.3, HTTP/3, the browser integrity check, Bot Fight Mode, email obfuscation and
              AI crawler blocking.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Full (strict) TLS.</strong> Cloudflare only connects to CloudFront when CloudFront presents a valid
              certificate for the domain.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>CloudFront</strong> holds an ACM certificate for both names, validated through records in Cloudflare
              and renewed automatically. It redirects plain HTTP to HTTPS.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>A CloudFront Function</strong> runs on every request. It refuses HTTPS requests that lack the secret
              header Cloudflare adds, so the site can't be reached around Cloudflare's edge. It redirects www to the apex
              and maps folder paths like <code>/resume</code> to <code>/resume/index.html</code>, because S3 has no index
              documents.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Managed policies</strong> set the caching and add security headers, including a one-year HSTS
              header. A missing page gets the site's own 404 page, with status 404.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>S3</strong> keeps the files in a private bucket with every public access setting blocked. Visitors
              only get files through CloudFront, which signs its reads with origin access control. Versioning keeps 30
              days of old files, so a bad deploy can be rolled back.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="iac" class="hiw-section wrap" aria-labelledby="iac-title">
        <div class="hiw-panel glass-panel">
          <div class="hiw-head">
            <h2 id="iac-title">Infrastructure as code</h2>
            <p>
              The site's AWS resources and DNS records live in OpenTofu, in three stacks whose AWS side and state sit in
              the site's own AWS account. CI applies two of them, and every role it manages stays under a ceiling only a
              person can move.
            </p>
          </div>
          <figure class="hiw-figure-inline">
            <Diagram data={iacSplit} minScale={DIAGRAM_MIN_SCALE} />
            <figcaption>A person sets the ceiling; CI works below it.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <ul class="hiw-points">
            <li>
              <strong>Three stacks, one state bucket.</strong> All three keep their state in one versioned, encrypted S3
              bucket in the site's account, locked with S3's native lock files. That bucket is the only part of the stacks
              made by hand, before anything else.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Site stack.</strong> The bucket, the distribution and its function, the certificate and the
              Cloudflare records. It holds no IAM.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>IAM stack.</strong> The deploy, plan and apply roles, and a Resource Group for the project. CI
              applies it before the site stack, so new permissions exist before the site needs them.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Bootstrap stack.</strong> The GitHub OIDC provider, a permissions boundary and the role that applies
              the IAM stack. A person applies it from a laptop with admin single sign-on, and only when the ceiling must
              move.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>The ceiling.</strong> Every role CI manages must carry that boundary, which caps it at the site
              bucket, the site state, CloudFront, ACM and read-only IAM. CI can't edit the boundary, the OIDC provider or
              the role it applies IAM with.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Trust.</strong> Each role trusts one GitHub job context, matched on GitHub's immutable OIDC subject,
              which survives renames. Pull requests get the read-only plan role, and the <code>infra-iam</code>,
              <code>infra</code> and <code>production</code> environments get the IAM, apply and deploy roles. All three
              environments take <code>main</code> only. Sessions last an hour, and no AWS keys are stored anywhere.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Cloudflare tokens.</strong> Two API tokens limited to this zone: a read-only one for plans, and one
              that can edit DNS and the request header rule for applies, held in the <code>infra</code> environment.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Tags.</strong> Resources carry <code>Project=portfolio</code> in AWS and in Cloudflare, so each
              console lists the project in one place.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="aws" class="hiw-section wrap" aria-labelledby="aws-title">
        <div class="hiw-panel glass-panel">
          <div class="hiw-head">
            <h2 id="aws-title">The AWS accounts</h2>
            <p>
              An AWS Organization with two accounts: one for identity, billing and audit, and one that runs only this
              site, under the organization's guardrails. Few ways in, all of them logged, and alerts that land in an inbox.
            </p>
          </div>
          <figure class="hiw-figure-inline">
            <Diagram data={awsOrg} minScale={DIAGRAM_MIN_SCALE} />
            <figcaption>Guardrails at the root bind the site's account; AWS never applies them to the management account.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <ul class="hiw-points">
            <li>
              <strong>Two accounts.</strong> The management account holds AWS Organizations, billing, IAM Identity Center
              and the organization trail. Everything the site runs on in AWS, from its bucket and distribution to its CI
              roles and OpenTofu state, lives in a member account, <code>portfolio</code>, in a Workloads organizational
              unit.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Guardrails.</strong> Service control policies at the organization root limit member accounts:
              regional API calls only in us-east-1 (global services excepted), new S3 buckets only in us-east-1, no
              stopping or deleting CloudTrail trails, the Config recorder, GuardDuty or Security Hub, and no leaving the
              organization or closing the account. The organization trail itself lives in the management account, where
              member accounts can't change it, and an alarm watches it for changes. A resource control policy lets only the organization's identities
              and AWS services reach S3 in member accounts. Neither applies to the management account, and SCPs don't limit
              AWS's own service-linked roles; that is one reason nothing runs in the management account.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Root users.</strong> The management account's root user signs in with a hardware security key and
              has no access keys, and an alarm fires if it is ever used. The site's account has no root password or access
              keys at all; the few tasks that need root run as short, task-scoped sessions started from the management
              account.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <figure class="hiw-figure-inline">
            <Diagram data={awsAccount} minScale={DIAGRAM_MIN_SCALE} />
            <figcaption>Who can act, what watches, and where the alerts go.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <ul class="hiw-points">
            <li>
              <strong>People.</strong> No IAM users in either account. People sign in through IAM Identity Center, with
              four-hour sessions.
            </li>
            <li><strong>CI.</strong> GitHub Actions uses the OIDC roles above, in the site's account, so no long-lived keys exist.</li>
            <li>
              <strong>Audit trail.</strong> An organization CloudTrail trail records sign-ins and management actions in both
              accounts and every Region, with log file validation, KMS encryption and a copy in CloudWatch Logs. Alarms on
              it email on root use, console sign-in without MFA, IAM policy changes and trail changes.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Threats and posture.</strong> Each account runs its own checks in us-east-1, where everything runs:
              GuardDuty emails high-severity findings, Security Hub checks against AWS Foundational Security Best
              Practices and CIS, AWS Config records changes against 18 rules of its own, and Inspector covers EC2,
              container images and Lambda. IAM Access Analyzer flags external and unused access across the organization.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Safe defaults.</strong> In both accounts, S3 Block Public Access is on account-wide, EBS volumes in
              us-east-1 are encrypted by default, public sharing of Systems Manager documents is blocked in every Region,
              and there are no default VPCs, so nothing gets a network by accident.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Cost.</strong> A $20 monthly budget for both accounts warns at 50%, 80% and 100% of actual spend and
              on forecast. Cost Anomaly Detection flags unusual spend, and a billing alarm fires above $10.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="cloudflare" class="hiw-section wrap" aria-labelledby="cloudflare-title">
        <div class="hiw-panel glass-panel">
          <div class="hiw-head">
            <h2 id="cloudflare-title">The Cloudflare account</h2>
            <p>Cloudflare holds the domain, its DNS and the edge in front of the site.</p>
          </div>
          <figure class="hiw-figure-inline">
            <Diagram data={cloudflareZone} minScale={DIAGRAM_MIN_SCALE} />
            <figcaption>Proxied for the web, DNS-only for the certificate and for mail.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <ul class="hiw-points">
            <li>
              <strong>Account.</strong> Sign-in uses two-factor authentication. The domain is registered with Cloudflare
              Registrar, and the zone runs on the Free plan.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>DNS.</strong> Cloudflare's nameservers serve the zone, signed with DNSSEC. Web records are proxied.
              Certificate validation and mail records are DNS-only, because ACM and mail servers must see the real values.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Edge.</strong> Full (strict) TLS to the origin, TLS 1.3, automatic HTTPS rewrites, HTTP/3 and Brotli.
              The browser integrity check, Bot Fight Mode, email address obfuscation and AI crawler blocking are on. A
              request header rule adds the secret header CloudFront checks, on HTTPS requests to the site's two names only.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Mail.</strong> The domain's mail runs on iCloud+, with SPF, DKIM and a DMARC policy of reject, which
              asks receiving servers to refuse mail that fakes the domain.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Automation.</strong> OpenTofu manages the site's records with the zone-scoped tokens. Tags are set
              with Cloudflare's own CLI, because the Terraform provider can't manage them yet.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="services" class="hiw-section wrap" aria-labelledby="services-title">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <h2 id="services-title">Every service</h2>
          <p>From the first edit to your screen.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hiw-services">
          {
            services.map((stage) => (
              <div class="hiw-stage glass-panel">
                <h3>{stage.title}</h3>
                <ul>
                  {stage.items.map((item) => (
                    <li>
                      <strong>{item.name}</strong> {item.role}
                    </li>
                  ))}
                </ul>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>
    <footer class="site-footer wrap">
      <span>© {site.fullName}</span>
      <span>{site.location}</span>
      <a href="#top">Back to top <span aria-hidden="true">↑</span></a>
    </footer>
  </div>
</Base>

<script>
  import { initScrollableHosts } from '../scripts/scrollable-hosts';
  import { initDiagramReveal } from '../scripts/diagram-reveal';

  initScrollableHosts();
  initDiagramReveal();
</script>
